# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored and never committed.
#
# These are consumed at CMAKE-CONFIGURE time, not at build time and not at
# runtime: examples/bench/CMakeLists.txt declares them as cache variables
# and compiles them into the image as PICO_HTTP_WIFI_SSID /
# PICO_HTTP_WIFI_PASSWORD string definitions. Two consequences follow, and
# both need checking:
#
#   1. Read the file in a subshell and configure the firmware tree explicitly:
#
#        (
#          set -a; . ./.env; set +a
#          cmake -S . -B build-firmware -G Ninja \
#            -DPICO_SDK_PATH="$PICO_SDK_PATH" -DPICO_BOARD=pico2_w \
#            -DPICO_LWIPOPTS_TARGET=pico_http_lwipopts_fallback \
#            -DPICO_HTTP_ENABLE_TESTS=OFF -DPICO_HTTP_ENABLE_EXAMPLES=ON \
#            -DPICO_HTTP_WIFI_SSID="$WIFI_SSID" \
#            -DPICO_HTTP_WIFI_PASSWORD="$WIFI_PASSWORD"
#          cmake --build build-firmware
#        )
#
#      python3 -m infra.pico_tooling device flash refuses an image whose cache
#      declares PICO_HTTP_WIFI_SSID but leaves it empty.
#
#   2. The values are baked into an existing build tree's cache. Changing them
#      needs a reconfigure; a stale cache silently produces firmware that
#      cannot join. Verify before flashing:
#
#        strings build-firmware/examples/bench/pico_http_bench.elf \
#          | grep -c "$WIFI_SSID"
#
# The two names below are the shell variables this file defines; the cmake
# cache variables they feed are namespaced PICO_HTTP_WIFI_*, like every other
# knob in the family. This file is the developer's, not a repository's, so
# one .env serves every repo on the machine.
#

WIFI_SSID=
WIFI_PASSWORD=
